  "body": "Current spec for `<meta application-name>` has this:\r\n\r\n> User agents may use the application name in UI in preference to the page's [title](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/semantics.html#the-title-element), since the title **might include status messages** and the like relevant to the status of the page at a particular moment in time instead of just being the name of the application.\r\n(emphasis mine)\r\n\r\nInserting notifications into the page title is really quite common, especially on social media sites, and certainly relevant for [WCAG SC 4.1.3: Status Messages](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG21/Understanding/status-messages), however I don't think changes in page title are reported to accessibility APIs (compared to changes in, say `<output>` elements which are live regions by default).\r\n\r\nThe current spec might mislead content authors to rely wholly on title changes to communicate status, which is not really \"accessibly supported\". I don't think this is a serious problem, but it does leave room for doubt.\r\n\r\nI know there is whatwg/html spec work being done about handling focus in \"single page apps\" or \"client side rerouting\" (whatever we want to call that). I think that changes in page title are relevant here. \r\n\r\nWould it not make some sense for ATs to announce such changes?\r\n",
